on/math-academy-id "q-225167" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u+v,\\,y=(u-v)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{4}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{4}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{4},$ we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,(u-v)^{2}) \\\\  & =(0+v,\\,(0-v)^{2}) \\\\  & =(v,\\,{v}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{4}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=v,\\,y={v}^{2},\\,1\\ge v\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y={t}^{2},\\,1\\ge t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhich is an upward-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{4}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(0,1)\\to (0+1,(0-1)^{2})=(1,1) \\\\  & T:(0,0)\\to (0+0,(0-0)^{2})=(0,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{4}$ is an arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,1)$ to $(0,0).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/d5238b62454c6422988e55f0f4ab9183387725fed037df2a688d351b568ea5c1.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-225167/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c9c1d-236d-571f-89be-d1c03f7c39aa"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-225167"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-225158/selection/f5804e8e19a71173763d65eb35222f80b8ad41886c1510c0e133c9289c3dce11" :answer/id #uuid "27d9afa5-0e4b-5544-90d4-89c913187bd0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1).$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-225158/selection/89d062808b648a7f7c5750edfd96feaac01c79b8c7d3decf93a44b57972a4b46" :answer/id #uuid "484e9977-09c1-5336-9340-45b1fe4ea94c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(1,1)$ to $(0,0).$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-225158/selection/c204cf4ef411a76294788daf2bb25a72ec8b9954a06bb52e9daefac1c4328893" :answer/id #uuid "57b9f3e6-7c57-51b1-8523-1b6ce871636c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1).$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-225158/selection/8c0ed4c20f12ac643fbfb9ab70017e49602c1414961609b90bd07698ad110b6b" :answer/id #uuid "0efb17e9-cf42-501b-af19-46eea80187e3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "An arc of an upward-opening parabola, traversed from  $(1,0)$ to $(0,1).$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-225158/selection/0fb893cb8b5f6abad3d4b72fa6adc58d5bf452c1450e745bfc7abd68c32a18b1" :answer/id #uuid "e324746b-878f-5c0d-ab43-30f434981b04" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "An arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,1)$ to $(1,0).$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-225158/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "36876614-8bbb-5fd0-8522-7e467b5f8791" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-225158/selection/c204cf4ef411a76294788daf2bb25a72ec8b9954a06bb52e9daefac1c4328893" :answer-field/choices ["q-225158/selection/f5804e8e19a71173763d65eb35222f80b8ad41886c1510c0e133c9289c3dce11" "q-225158/selection/89d062808b648a7f7c5750edfd96feaac01c79b8c7d3decf93a44b57972a4b46" "q-225158/selection/c204cf4ef411a76294788daf2bb25a72ec8b9954a06bb52e9daefac1c4328893" "q-225158/selection/8c0ed4c20f12ac643fbfb9ab70017e49602c1414961609b90bd07698ad110b6b" "q-225158/selection/0fb893cb8b5f6abad3d4b72fa6adc58d5bf452c1450e745bfc7abd68c32a18b1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-225158" :question/id #uuid "debba30b-e66b-5767-8579-12003aa3e4fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-225158" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u-v,\\,y=u(u-{v}^{3}).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side ${S}_{1}$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side ${S}_{1}.$\n\nFor the side ${S}_{1},$ we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u-v,\\,u(u-{v}^{3})) \\\\  & =(u-0,\\,u(u-{0}^{3})) \\\\  & =(u,\\,{u}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of ${S}_{1}$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=u,\\,y={u}^{2},\\,0\\le u\\le 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\,y(t)={t}^{2},\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=y\\,⇒\\,y={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhich is a upward-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of ${S}_{1}$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(0,0)\\to (0-0,\\,0(0-{0}^{3}))=(0,0) \\\\  & T:(1,0)\\to (1-0,\\,1(1-{0}^{3}))=(1,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${S}_{1}$ is an arc of a upward-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/7aa56f8bba41a352a02c6d26554c417e7844cd60c28078da51cbf16a3220e57d.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-225158/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c9c1d-236d-571f-89be-d1c03f7c39aa"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-225158"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-160635/selection/efbec158473bf1faa9e449b512aca90115a1909cf86ca0b72dbf4f804b6d8ebd" :answer/id #uuid "85fccc33-25d8-54ec-81de-41fe9f544ed6" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/2523e3cb51fea9c58b2076d69c89d21503341c6cbd4ca0db56d6b92455a40340.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160635/selection/7c8c2d32345c2e5b18726e8dd98d4460e9745fa1f9ffc6372d01bffeeecdacf0" :answer/id #uuid "218f8e2c-f12f-5f51-9e1a-64e40893532d" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/be160b4308b9bc676e1fb8033a1200e5af818007cc1f48661339b1c8d29a58db.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160635/selection/89f19b3684761b872f5917f178567c6643436390f03b62516d25fdc226c6b9cf" :answer/id #uuid "a0aa560c-75ce-50b5-8791-8a87cb19cedb" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/aa0d4fc0d276619b071a32fe727e7647ff29700bb34ec840a93cacc9088db1af.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160635/selection/e10004ea377c8acb7addac82362a51156c163de115ba79bf96154fbee72c33da" :answer/id #uuid "e949e924-25e9-55dd-9821-64e9af4cda8a" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/a687a8434e9643eabcdf3e1600a28eb805f35fd7821936effcb28677366e91ef.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160635/selection/b8b0c24bf3d01fdb8bd5e9a2f2689b27e177deba159bd6d8b31f974c8909e8e0" :answer/id #uuid "783bab69-6e47-5681-8622-e86a86d59f12" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/5ced1d3826236965d91ac2de999398e4f7390f0fbf84619ae87c1d2a96370625.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160635/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "bf72fdca-ab6c-5c93-b329-d3bf42839303" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-160635/selection/b8b0c24bf3d01fdb8bd5e9a2f2689b27e177deba159bd6d8b31f974c8909e8e0" :answer-field/choices ["q-160635/selection/efbec158473bf1faa9e449b512aca90115a1909cf86ca0b72dbf4f804b6d8ebd" "q-160635/selection/7c8c2d32345c2e5b18726e8dd98d4460e9745fa1f9ffc6372d01bffeeecdacf0" "q-160635/selection/89f19b3684761b872f5917f178567c6643436390f03b62516d25fdc226c6b9cf" "q-160635/selection/e10004ea377c8acb7addac82362a51156c163de115ba79bf96154fbee72c33da" "q-160635/selection/b8b0c24bf3d01fdb8bd5e9a2f2689b27e177deba159bd6d8b31f974c8909e8e0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-160635" :question/id #uuid "cf2b710f-4869-56c7-9e96-7189ff69d504" :question/math-academy-id "q-160635" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=uv,\\,y=-{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u(0),\\,-{0}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,0), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(1v,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,-{v}^{2}), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a downward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u(1),\\,-{1}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u,\\,-1), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(1,-1)$ to the point $(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0v,\\,-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,-{v}^{2}), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(0,-1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/5ced1d3826236965d91ac2de999398e4f7390f0fbf84619ae87c1d2a96370625.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-160635/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "efb1958d-6ee8-592e-b503-7b15e6e7b056"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-160635"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-160353"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=v,\\,y=u(1+{v}^{2}).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,u(1+{0}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,u), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(0,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,1(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,1+{v}^{2}), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,2)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(1,\\,u(1+{1}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(1,\\,2u), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(1,2)$ to the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,u(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,0(1+{v}^{2})) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/468a2bf9e981f18b656fe1b5a023f98d1f5c990880caf5f9908c05cc3fe2d197.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-160629/selection/4887d3695fe89e8c74aa2a34840a0dccee1ba90a1d66cbe264ae50506a85b338" :answer/id #uuid "71d1ed8c-f574-5f4c-91e1-ef1653ce4cf2" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/6114f6e6fec1d7d0a4d41cfba15f3fa5b2a28ef5c288ad4848d6f0fab1ca01a9.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160629/selection/f981ef94b341f7fc74115982212c4bac8e6f038e1c9a2fa7f254bf3ef1adb7a2" :answer/id #uuid "c25848cf-d0f1-5a68-990b-0aa101615aa8" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/cdd115f6defaabc72aaaeb8a6a1642e088860f281a847acacdb635f71a26be08.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160629/selection/2dd9443d53da05e7583051cbe53fddc9c54c8919f9e33fbfe58cf60752dfa2a0" :answer/id #uuid "00d3bdcf-2cf1-5cb6-b1e1-d53be495bbc8" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/e7c699d1d96e60f031a6fb006e4ae5ae1abd9898f574ab075d4e4a15a4958634.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160629/selection/890ae7ebcfe57c58dcf22113d7d645a5097f52d5e33b54fa696a4287c9abea69" :answer/id #uuid "1df2575e-aec1-554c-8287-4cafca457b64" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/225339084f34f53e3a406bb8804d31c0fb5f288c6d4456125926a0d0b1116122.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160629/selection/df99a5a9b0dd80b53365ba662fdd83939be2e9a18d1ad85b105ab71f6fb6a4f2" :answer/id #uuid "7bf3704c-b614-5b63-8d9d-68fbda499fdf" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/fc78afa81f267849dd5546919074c8bfcab6b35430358abaa6e2c9567c599ee0.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160629/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "486447bc-b244-52c6-bfab-545563a149c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-160629/selection/4887d3695fe89e8c74aa2a34840a0dccee1ba90a1d66cbe264ae50506a85b338" :answer-field/choices ["q-160629/selection/4887d3695fe89e8c74aa2a34840a0dccee1ba90a1d66cbe264ae50506a85b338" "q-160629/selection/f981ef94b341f7fc74115982212c4bac8e6f038e1c9a2fa7f254bf3ef1adb7a2" "q-160629/selection/2dd9443d53da05e7583051cbe53fddc9c54c8919f9e33fbfe58cf60752dfa2a0" "q-160629/selection/890ae7ebcfe57c58dcf22113d7d645a5097f52d5e33b54fa696a4287c9abea69" "q-160629/selection/df99a5a9b0dd80b53365ba662fdd83939be2e9a18d1ad85b105ab71f6fb6a4f2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-160629" :question/id #uuid "4d8b7a38-c4a6-5f88-ab7d-5f69499c2741" :question/math-academy-id "q-160629" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u+v,\\,y={v}^{2}-{u}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,{v}^{2}-{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u+0,\\,{0}^{2}-{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u,\\,-{u}^{2}), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a downward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,{v}^{2}-{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(1+v,\\,{v}^{2}-{1}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(v+1,\\,{v}^{2}-1), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,-1)$ to the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,{v}^{2}-{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u+1,\\,{1}^{2}-{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(u+1,\\,1-{u}^{2}), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a downward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u+v,\\,{v}^{2}-{u}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0+v,\\,{v}^{2}-{0}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,{v}^{2}), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/6114f6e6fec1d7d0a4d41cfba15f3fa5b2a28ef5c288ad4848d6f0fab1ca01a9.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-160629/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "efb1958d-6ee8-592e-b503-7b15e6e7b056"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-160629"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-160628/selection/ec1564174a4f450f3f01fcfdff8b0ea639725b0b10b5ff77915fdc0ea797bc56" :answer/id #uuid "e8ebf506-2153-5b1e-b0ce-d1d01f072ad0" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/941f34cbed3f3d6a9957b8520b36d6cbdc20c6241d2e407381ac86d3241a3349.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160628/selection/f56b62cb0796897dc5721a773660aeb76ce7f6d92a8b2984db2ef90d6581053d" :answer/id #uuid "9b0bc948-4df2-553b-b7fc-2f673475b1c4" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/7f41dd7a042f38707d5b0ad8e2daf4612a92d26de34c8eff646b929de420e773.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160628/selection/ac8cffcc1ad14a4949de425bc43ffa3102673cf8857a2c11f81cb713eade9061" :answer/id #uuid "f7c08be7-f7ad-51ec-8ccf-d6edb44cf77a" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/6d514d79a788e4512370310eab371dc334e37fc20aaffdf3ad93ebe632b84d4b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160628/selection/3d6ee402a2db42046c76c4f34f0d78392035088d9b6cb6ce63d32b669c793952" :answer/id #uuid "3d7f41eb-3ffb-5660-892b-9cf3a0dc01c5" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/4ee5f1df8f74ca6412d4f02fba7258b2b672a8bc80ef13617d4b327f8859e007.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160628/selection/617c46d8076f2357c4b3e02a167665a5dee33575670711dd5eda908a73df97a3" :answer/id #uuid "584cfc04-bc2f-5698-8b0b-41b3504722be" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/662c1937641b84653920b9c4e8cfac8168470d077641d001ebd258a0ffff34ac.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160628/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f2bd6931-71ed-5c69-b6c9-0e5f5f65fb4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-160628/selection/f56b62cb0796897dc5721a773660aeb76ce7f6d92a8b2984db2ef90d6581053d" :answer-field/choices ["q-160628/selection/ec1564174a4f450f3f01fcfdff8b0ea639725b0b10b5ff77915fdc0ea797bc56" "q-160628/selection/f56b62cb0796897dc5721a773660aeb76ce7f6d92a8b2984db2ef90d6581053d" "q-160628/selection/ac8cffcc1ad14a4949de425bc43ffa3102673cf8857a2c11f81cb713eade9061" "q-160628/selection/3d6ee402a2db42046c76c4f34f0d78392035088d9b6cb6ce63d32b669c793952" "q-160628/selection/617c46d8076f2357c4b3e02a167665a5dee33575670711dd5eda908a73df97a3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-160628" :question/id #uuid "6b264343-8b82-57fd-9f36-893b59fb2172" :question/math-academy-id "q-160628" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=-u,\\,y={u}^{2}-{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-{0}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(-1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-1,\\,{1}^{2}-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-1,\\,1-{v}^{2}), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(-1,1)$ to the point $(-1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-{1}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-1), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(-1,0)$ to the point $(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(-u,\\,{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(-0,\\,{0}^{2}-{v}^{2}) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,-{v}^{2}), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(0,-1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/7f41dd7a042f38707d5b0ad8e2daf4612a92d26de34c8eff646b929de420e773.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-160628/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "efb1958d-6ee8-592e-b503-7b15e6e7b056"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-160628"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-160641/selection/90e65a730fb636c0625a1e685d653f0130f08a35d7b16e260d68baaea319da4a" :answer/id #uuid "802fbc5b-f77e-5cc7-8925-b660cc4d1954" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/c91246add81f732ebca0ed0de3a32de9c6310bd889b890746374232bc2c25700.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160641/selection/449368467c70645b699a146d21cde04bd9b3352a1b953331fc06bf386919ba8e" :answer/id #uuid "0f0567ed-5707-54da-bfe8-21a0c04456a2" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/25098bb3cdab08abbfcc68e2a703e38df2abc07e562f6bfddee9e219135933e0.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160641/selection/8ef3723d63884e17b7648d3f2f978c894ff247f492b230340adc8aef178dc491" :answer/id #uuid "36d3c81b-e2bb-5259-be1d-0e09cb46af70" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/589601eccdc398c685c3d595689be60a3482df49e592292a620e9380b0664419.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160641/selection/f7d7136ac3f98472f680e0f17c00d33acac62efbb70ac085567b8fa32b288b0d" :answer/id #uuid "cf220dde-6566-5bf4-80fb-d0ea9c27089e" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/e9bbbef3a9ba9bbb3e305201281c82a46fe139972451686c8571cfc4d8b9fd26.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160641/selection/8a5b23dd29bba3bc9b26570efbd7834b8b8cb2677c75918d213362d9175df804" :answer/id #uuid "2b4ff75d-0561-56fd-adc9-1fbadd8df7b3" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/1eeaf3fc507fa7cb5ef5e28e05abf76f113e8253872446bc69c1640022ec780e.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160641/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "50a07a9e-3961-54ab-9dcf-db2c2e13e498" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-160641/selection/90e65a730fb636c0625a1e685d653f0130f08a35d7b16e260d68baaea319da4a" :answer-field/choices ["q-160641/selection/90e65a730fb636c0625a1e685d653f0130f08a35d7b16e260d68baaea319da4a" "q-160641/selection/449368467c70645b699a146d21cde04bd9b3352a1b953331fc06bf386919ba8e" "q-160641/selection/8ef3723d63884e17b7648d3f2f978c894ff247f492b230340adc8aef178dc491" "q-160641/selection/f7d7136ac3f98472f680e0f17c00d33acac62efbb70ac085567b8fa32b288b0d" "q-160641/selection/8a5b23dd29bba3bc9b26570efbd7834b8b8cb2677c75918d213362d9175df804"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-160641" :question/id #uuid "69f39331-db7f-52d8-999c-05736a898b72" :question/math-academy-id "q-160641" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx=u(v-u),\\,y=-u.\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$*" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $T$ has no critical points in the interior of $Δ.$ So, to map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{1},$ where we have $v=0$ and $0\\le u\\le 1.$ Substituting $v=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u(v-u),\\,-u) &  \\\\  & =(u(0-u),\\,-u) &  \\\\  & =(-{u}^{2},\\,-u), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a left-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(-1,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{2}.$ Here, we have $u=1$ and $0\\le v\\le 1.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u(v-u),\\,-u) &  \\\\  & =(1(v-1),\\,-1) &  \\\\  & =(v-1,\\,-1), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a horizontal line segment traversed from the point $(-1,-1)$ to the point $(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{3}.$ Here, we have $v=1$ and $1\\ge u\\ge 0.$ Substituting $v=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u(v-u),\\,-u) &  \\\\  & =(u(1-u),\\,-u) &  \\\\  & =(u-{u}^{2},\\,-u), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a left-opening parabola traversed from the point $(0,-1)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side ${S}_{4}.$ Here, we have $u=0$ and $1\\ge v\\ge 0.$ Substituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(u(v-u),\\,-u) &  \\\\  & =(0(v-0),\\,-0) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is the point $(0,0).$\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $Δ:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/c91246add81f732ebca0ed0de3a32de9c6310bd889b890746374232bc2c25700.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-160641/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "efb1958d-6ee8-592e-b503-7b15e6e7b056"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-160641"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-15087"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\n             \n\nConsider the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx = u - v^2, \\qquad y = 2v.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side $S_4$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is given by\n\n$$\nx = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{x(t)}, \\qquad y=2t, \\qquad 1 \\geq t \\geq 0.\n$$\n\nFind the function $x(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side $S_4.$\n\nFor the side $S_4,$ we have $u = 0$ and $1 \\geq v \\geq 0.$ Substituting $u = 0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u-{v}^{2},\\,2v) \\\\  & =(0-{v}^{2},\\,2v) \\\\  & =(-{v}^{2},\\,2v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of $S_4$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx = -v^2, \\qquad y=2v, \\qquad 1 \\geq v \\geq 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-t^2}, \\qquad y(t)=2t, \\qquad 1 \\geq t \\geq 0.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-15066"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\n             \n\nConsider the square region $\\Delta$ and its **directed** boundary $\\partial\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, where $\\Delta$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx = 2u-3v, \\qquad y=v^2-3u.\n$$\n\nThe image of the side $S_3$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a **directed** line segment. Find the endpoints of this segment, stated in the order that they are traversed." :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side $S_3.$\n\nFor the side $S_3,$ we have $v=1$ and $1 \\geq u \\geq 0.$ Substituting $v = 1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(2u-3v,\\,{v}^{2}-3u) \\\\  & =(2u-3(1),\\,{1}^{2}-3u) \\\\  & =(2u-3,1-3u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of $S_3$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx=2u-3, \\qquad y=1-3u, \\qquad 1 \\geq u \\geq 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = 2t-3, \\qquad y(t) = 1-3t, \\qquad 1 \\geq t \\geq 0.\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment with endpoints $(-1,-2)$ and $(-3,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/1583f8d3ad69b8d5e3ac051eeb50b55860737a60d462d126ae5dc2b49c3ea155.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-15064"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/33e48fa5bdbb034bcf489e038ccf80fc5f67baabeccacc1b7abd741183ebf05d.png)\n\n             \n\nConsider the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx = ( u + v)^2, \\qquad y = \\dfrac{u}{v+1}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the side $S_1$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the side $S_1.$\n\nFor the side $S_1,$ we have $v = 0$ and $0 \\leq u \\leq 1.$ Substituting $v = 0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =((u+v)^{2},\\,\\frac{u}{v+1}) \\\\  & =((u+0)^{2},\\,\\frac{u}{0+1}) \\\\  & =({u}^{2},\\,u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the image of $S_1$ can be parametrized by\n\n$$\nx = u^2, \\qquad y = u, \\qquad 0 \\leq u \\leq 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWriting this in the usual notation, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t^2, \\qquad y(t) = t, \\qquad 0 \\leq t \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nThese are parametric equations of a parabolic arc. Notice that if we eliminate $t$ from the equations, we get\n\n$$\ny^2=x\n$$\n\nwhich is a right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0).$\n\nMoreover, the endpoints of $S_1$ are mapped to the $xy$-plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T:(0,0)\\to ((0+0)^{2},\\,\\frac{0}{0+1})=(0,0) \\\\  & T:(1,0)\\to ((1+0)^{2},\\,\\frac{1}{0+1})=(1,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of $S_1$ is an arc of a right-opening parabola, traversed from $(0,0)$ to $(1,1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/7f94f6fef873efd64d9da9e015e0056944b63846d741e12937507205b118638b.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-15065"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/5f91cc8b30f9b764021cd84c374a328c7ffb1287a9f0dc6a211a3fadfac2fdbd.png)\n\n             \n\nConsider the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta  = \\big\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0\\leq v \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nx = uv, \\qquad y = u(u+v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points in the interior of $\\Delta.$ So, to map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $\\Delta$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the side $S_1,$ where we have $v = 0$ and $0 \\leq u \\leq 1.$ Substituting $v = 0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,u(u+v)) &  \\\\  & =(u(0),\\,u(u+0)) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,{u}^{2}), & 0\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a vertical line segment traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(0,1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side $S_2.$ Here, we have $u = 1$ and $0 \\leq v \\leq 1.$ Substituting $u = 1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,u(u+v)) &  \\\\  & =(1(v),\\,1(1+v)) &  \\\\  & =(v,\\,1+v), & 0\\le v\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line segment traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(1,2)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side $S_3.$ Here, we have $v = 1$ and $1 \\geq u \\geq 0.$ Substituting $v = 1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,u(u+v)) &  \\\\  & =(u(1),\\,u(u+1)) &  \\\\  & =(u,\\,{u}^{2}+u), & 1\\ge u\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola traversed from the point $(1,2)$ to the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Consider the image of the side $S_4.$ Here, we have $u = 0$ and $1 \\geq v \\geq 0.$ Substituting $u = 0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) &  \\\\  & =(uv,\\,u(u+v)) &  \\\\  & =(0v,\\,0(0+v)) &  \\\\  & =(0,\\,0), & 1\\ge v\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThis is the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nDrawing the images of the sides of our square, we obtain the following image of $\\Delta{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14046771/assets/793412e250ac4f26966293771306f28bb037685dd7886dc386706f23dcaee8af.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
